 To force a text field to display columns in Adobe JavaScript (AJAX), you can use the `textFlow` and `ParagraphFormat` classes to create and format multiple lines of text, and then add those lines to the text field as separate paragraphs. Here's an example of how you can implement this:

```javascript
var textField = app.activeDocument.textFrames.item(0); // Replace with your text frame
var text = "Column 1\nColumn 2\nColumn 3\nColumn 4"; // Replace with your text

var textFlow = new TextFlow(text);
var lines = textFlow.lines;
var width = textField.width; // Set the width of the columns
var columns = Math.floor(width / lines[0].width); // Calculate the number of columns

for (var i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
  var start = i * columns;
  var end = start + columns;
  if (end > lines.length) end = lines.length;

  var paragraph = lines[start];
  paragraph.paragraphs[0].appliedParagraphFormat.alignment = TextAlignment.JUSTIFY;
  paragraph.startIndent = 0;
  paragraph.endIndent = 0;
  paragraph.paragraphs[0].appliedParagraphFormat.leftIndent = width / columns;

  if (end < lines.length) {
    var nextParagraph = lines[end].paragraphs[0];
    nextParagraph.paragraphs[0].appliedParagraphFormat.leftIndent = 0;
  }

  textField.appendTextFlow(paragraph);
}
```

This script will create a justified text flow with the specified number of columns and append it to the text frame. Make sure to replace `app.activeDocument.textFrames.item(0)` with your text frame, and modify the `text` variable to match your desired content.

